## Part 1: How Target Ranking Works (Step by Step)

### The Big Picture

We take the licence area, divide it into a grid of **10,000 squares** (100 × 100). Each square is called a **cell**. We score every cell from 0 to 100 for "how promising is this spot for gold", then pick the best few as drill targets.

---

### Step 1 — Score Each Cell on 5 Clues

For every cell on the grid, we measure **5 things** (called "evidence layers"). Each clue gets a score between 0 and 1 (where 1 = very promising), and each clue has a **weight** that says how much we trust it.

| # | Clue | Full Name / What It Means | Weight | Why This Weight? |
|---|---|---|---:|---|
| 1 | **Geochemistry** | Soil samples tested in a lab. Gold and arsenic leak into surrounding soil from a buried deposit. High readings = something was down there. | **30%** | Most direct sign a gold system existed |
| 2 | **Structure** | Cracks, faults and shears underground — the "plumbing" that gold travels through. Detected as sharp edges in magnetic readings. | **25%** | Gold deposits sit on these structures |
| 3 | **Magnetics** | How magnetic the ground is. Gold chemistry **destroys** magnetic minerals, so a magnetic **dip** (low reading) is actually a good sign. This is the only **inverted** clue — low = good. | **20%** | Indicates the right chemistry happened |
| 4 | **Rock Type (Lithology)** | Some rocks host gold well, others don't. Ancient volcanic rock = high score. Loose surface soil/gravel = low score. | **15%** | Gold only forms in certain rock types |
| 5 | **Nearby Known Deposits** | Gold clusters together. Being near a known mine means the wider geology is right. | **10%** | Lowest weight because it partly measures where people already looked, not where gold actually is |

**The calculation:** Multiply each clue by its weight → add them up → multiply by 100 = the cell's score out of 100.

> **Example:** A cell scoring 0.9 on geochemistry and 0.8 on structure gets:
> (0.9 × 0.30) + (0.8 × 0.25) + ... = weighted sum → × 100 = score

> [!IMPORTANT]
> **If a project is missing a clue** (e.g. no geochemistry data), the weights automatically re-adjust so the remaining clues still add up to 100%. The project isn't penalised — it just scores on whatever evidence it has.

---

### Step 2 — Make the Score Explainable (SHAP)

The scores from Step 1 are fed into a tool called **XGBoost** (a standard machine-learning tool), which learns to copy our own arithmetic. It doesn't discover anything new.

Then a technique called **SHAP** (SHapley Additive exPlanations — a method that breaks down a score into contributions) answers: *"Of this cell's score of 84, how much came from geochemistry? How much from structure?"*

This is how a geologist can see **why** a spot ranked highly — not just trust a number.

> [!NOTE]
> The machine learning exists purely to make our own reasoning inspectable. It contributes no geology of its own.

---

### Step 3 — Rule Out No-Go Ground (Protected Areas)

Any cell inside a **national park or protected reserve** is **forced to zero**. Not reduced — **zeroed**.

A protected area is a legal fact, not a geological opinion. No amount of promising geology is allowed to outvote it.

---

### Step 4 — Turn 10,000 Cells Into a Shortlist

Three sub-steps produce the final target list:

#### 4a. Filter
A cell qualifies only if it meets **BOTH** conditions:
- It's in the **top 10%** of all cells
- It scores at least **60 out of 100**

> [!IMPORTANT]
> If nothing clears the bar, the system returns **no targets at all**. A weak project gets an honest "nothing here" rather than five mediocre suggestions presented as good ones.

#### 4b. Group (Cluster)
One real deposit makes a whole patch of neighbouring cells look good. Listing the top 5 cells individually would often mean listing 5 cells sitting on the **same** anomaly — one target reported 5 times.

So neighbouring high-scoring cells are grouped into clusters using **HDBSCAN** (Hierarchical Density-Based Spatial Clustering of Applications with Noise — a method that finds natural groupings of different sizes). Each cluster becomes one target.

#### 4c. Rank
Each cluster gets:
- A **location** (pulled toward its strongest cells, not just the geographic middle)
- A **score** (its single best cell's score)
- A **mean score** (average across all cells in the cluster)
- A **cell count** (how many cells it covers)
- A **depth estimate** (how far down the source might be, from magnetic data using Euler deconvolution)

Clusters are **sorted by their best cell's score**. The **top 5** become the ranked drill targets.

---

### Summary Flow

```
User's survey data + Public data
        ↓
Grid the licence area (100×100 cells)
        ↓
Score each cell on 5 clues (weighted sum → 0-100)
        ↓
Feed to XGBoost → SHAP breaks down "why"
        ↓
Zero out protected areas
        ↓
Filter: top 10% AND ≥60 score
        ↓
Cluster with HDBSCAN (group neighbouring cells)
        ↓
Rank clusters by peak score → Top 5 = Drill Targets
```

